{"causes": ["The Athenian defeat at the Battle of Notium in 407 BCE created a leadership vacuum and public demand for new military commanders.", "The ongoing Peloponnesian War necessitated the appointment of experienced strategos like Erasinides to maintain Athenian naval supremacy.", "Alcibiades' political downfall and exile in 407 BCE directly led to the Athenian assembly seeking ten new generals, including Erasinides."], "effects": ["Erasinides' participation in the Battle of Arginusae contributed to a significant, albeit short-lived, Athenian naval victory over Sparta in 406 BCE.", "His subsequent trial and execution, alongside other generals, severely depleted Athens' experienced military leadership, weakening its war effort.", "The Arginusae trial established a notorious precedent for Athenian democratic overreach and judicial injustice, debated by historians for centuries."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "athenian-assembly", "sourceName": "Athenian Assembly", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "erasinides", "targetName": "Erasinides", "context": "The Athenian Assembly appointed Erasinides as one of ten strategoi in 407 BCE after the Battle of Notium."}, {"sourceSlug": "erasinides", "sourceName": "Erasinides", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-arginusae", "targetName": "Battle of Arginusae", "context": "Erasinides served as one of the eight Athenian strategoi during the decisive naval victory at the Battle of Arginusae in 406 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "erasinides", "sourceName": "Erasinides", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "athens", "targetName": "Athens", "context": "Erasinides was tried and executed in Athens following the controversy surrounding the Battle of Arginusae."}, {"sourceSlug": "arginusae-trial", "sourceName": "Arginusae Trial", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "erasinides", "targetName": "Erasinides", "context": "The Arginusae Trial, which condemned the victorious generals for failing to rescue survivors, directly led to Erasinides' execution in 405 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "peloponnesian-war", "sourceName": "Peloponnesian War", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "battle-of-arginusae", "targetName": "Battle of Arginusae", "context": "The Battle of Arginusae, in which Erasinides commanded, was a major naval engagement during the latter phase of the Peloponnesian War."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Place of Trial and Execution"}, {"name": "Arginusae Islands, Turkey", "role": "Site of Naval Victory"}, {"name": "Aegean Sea, Global", "role": "Theater of Operations"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Erasinides", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "erasinides"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1347999", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erasinides"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Erasinides, as a commander in the Battle of Arginusae and a victim of the subsequent trial, highlights the severe instability of Athenian democracy in crisis and the critical weakening of its military leadership, directly contributing to its eventual defeat in the Peloponnesian War.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-15T14:43:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29424419974", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Erasinides was one of the ten commanders appointed to supersede Alcibiades after the Battle of Notium in 407 BCE.", "newValue": "Erasinides, an Athenian strategos (general) during the latter stages of the Peloponnesian War, rose to prominence in 407 BCE following the disastrous defeat at Notium, which led to the recall and eventual exile of the celebrated commander Alcibiades. He was one of ten generals appointed by the Athenian assembly to replace Alcibiades and lead the Athenian fleet, a testament to the city's urgent need for new leadership amidst escalating conflict with Sparta. His career, though brief, encapsulates the volatile nature of Athenian democracy and its severe consequences for military commanders in times of crisis, ultimately culminating in his execution.

His most significant command came in 406 BCE at the pivotal Battle of Arginusae, where he served as one of the eight strategoi who achieved a decisive naval victory over the Spartan fleet under Callicratidas. This engagement, fought near the Arginusae islands, was a major tactical success for Athens, breaking the Spartan blockade of Mytilene and demonstrating the resilience of the Athenian navy. However, the subsequent failure to rescue Athenian sailors from shipwrecks during a sudden storm after the battle ignited a fierce political and legal storm back in Athens, setting the stage for a dramatic and unprecedented trial.

